  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/18-toothcarps-killis-and-livebearers/xiphophorus-nezahualcoyotl-2/",
      "html": "<p>X. nezahualcoyotl was formerly considered a local variant of X. montezumae. The name refers to Nezahualcoyotl, the poet-philosopher emperor of Tezcoco (Texcoco), regarded as equal to Montezuma, monarch of the Aztecs in the Aztec Triple Alliance, because the species is considered a sister taxon of X. montezumae.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/18-toothcarps-killis-and-livebearers/xiphophorus-nezahualcoyotl-2/",
      "html": "<p>X. nezahualcoyotl is found only in the headwaters of some rivers in the state of San Luis Potosí in Mexico.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "habitat",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/18-toothcarps-killis-and-livebearers/xiphophorus-nezahualcoyotl-2/",
      "html": "<p>The water there is clear, the soil stony, and the temperatures moderate (22-24°C).</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "maintenance",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/18-toothcarps-killis-and-livebearers/xiphophorus-nezahualcoyotl-2/",
      "html": "<p>Care of this small-bodied swordtail is not very difficult, but care must be taken to maintain good water hygiene.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "dimorphism",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/18-toothcarps-killis-and-livebearers/xiphophorus-nezahualcoyotl-2/",
      "html": "<p>Body length rarely exceeds 4 cm (the largest wild-caught male measured had 48 mm standard length), plus fins. The species may appear with or without black mottling. The black speckling is linked to the Y-chromosome, so it is inherited from the father. In the young adults shown, the sword of the males is still relatively short.</p>"
    }
  ],
